# Exploring an AI-augmented strategic design practice

## Purpose

Share first to frame the problem, show the current workflow challenge and create curiosity.

## Core message

My work increasingly involves complex CX problems that sit across customer strategy, channel experience, CRM adoption, information architecture, service information, data quality, governance, stakeholder alignment and delivery planning.

These areas are highly connected, but they are often managed through separate artefacts, systems and conversations. To move work forward, I often need to manually synthesise the problem, the decision needed, the evidence available, the assumptions being made, the stakeholders who need to align, the artefact that will help, and what should become reusable.

This synthesis is valuable, but it is not yet structured as a consistent AI-augmented strategic design practice that can be repeated, explained, improved or shared with the team.

## Current-state workflow

Complex CX issue emerges
        ↓
Inputs arrive through multiple disconnected places
Meetings / strategy decks / Confluence / Jira / data outputs /
stakeholder conversations / service issues / delivery pressures
        ↓
I manually synthesise the system
Customer needs + business goals + service logic + channel implications +
CRM/data constraints + stakeholder needs + delivery activity
        ↓
I clarify what the work is really about
Problem / decision needed / evidence / assumptions / caveats /
stakeholders / risks / useful artefact
        ↓
A strategic output is created
Deck / Confluence page / plan / pitch / framework / roadmap /
decision tool / workshop artefact
        ↓
Stakeholders align around the artefact
But some of the reusable decision logic remains implicit
        ↓
The next complex issue emerges
Context and reasoning need to be partly rebuilt

## Development opportunity

This FY, I want to explore how AI-agentic ways of working can help me augment this practice: not by replacing judgement, but by improving how I frame problems, synthesise complexity, test options, identify blind spots, avoid unproductive rabbit holes, create reusable artefacts and support clearer strategic decisions.

I also want to use this as a way to strengthen one of the most important parts of strategic work: bringing people along the journey. For me, this means using AI not only to produce better outputs, but to improve how I explain the logic of the work, make options easier to compare, surface assumptions earlier and create artefacts that help stakeholders understand why a direction is being recommended.

## Shift I want to make

Personal synthesis held mostly in my head
        ↓
Repeatable AI-augmented strategic design practice

This would help me improve my own effectiveness while creating learning that can support the broader team capability goal around experimentation, prototyping and new ways of working.
